{"actionsRun":["rm -rf .peer-lab/spawn-worktree lab-repo && git init -q -b feat/T27-Magaza-temizlik lab-repo && git -C lab-repo -c user.name=lab -c user.email=lab commit -q --allow-empty -m init","cat peer-sessions/SKILL.md; ls peer-sessions peer-sessions/*","cat peer-sessions/references/placement.md | head -80","python3 peer-sessions/scripts/spawn-fleet.py --placement workspace worker-1:lab-repo"],"sessionNames":["worker-1"],"tellUser":"One Claude peer session, worker-1, is running in lab-repo/. It opened in its own herdr workspace (wF1, pane wF1:p1) and was ready right away. I haven't sent it a brief yet. Drive it with `herdr agent prompt worker-1 <text>` and read replies with `herdr agent wait worker-1` / `herdr agent read worker-1`. On this harness, SendMessage may only reach agents started through the Agent tool, so it may not reach worker-1.

The session will keep running, and keep using resources, until you ask me to stop it. Closing the workspace does not stop the claude process, and the spawn script's teardown has no kill line. To stop it, first get worker-1's PID from `python3 ~/.claude/skills/peer-sessions/scripts/peer-addr.py --name worker-1` and `kill <pid>`. Then run `herdr workspace close wF1` and rerun peer-addr.py to confirm it's gone.

To resume after a reboot: `cd lab-repo && claude --resume worker-1`."}
